
  [1mUpgrading from bounded to architectural.[0m The ratchet only goes one way, so I won't downgrade this later. That means:
  clarifying questions, 2-3 approaches with trade-offs, a sectioned design you approve section by section, and a written
  spec at [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/[39m before any code. No implementation until you've approved that.

  First question — it's the one everything else hangs off.

  Right now [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m and never calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. So the
  identifier either originates on the server or on the client, and that decision is the expensive one:

  - [1mServer-issued[0m — the login response carries the real account ID (and normally a session token). This is the only
    version where the ID actually attests that the user authenticated; the client just stores what it was given. It's
    also the one that's hard to change later, because every consumer ends up shaped around the response. But it means
    the design depends on a backend contract that doesn't exist yet in this repo, so we'd be designing against an
    assumed response shape.
  - [1mClient-generated[0m — the client mints and persists an ID itself. Nothing external is needed and it works today against
    the stub. But a client-minted value is self-asserted: it can be edited by anyone with devtools, so it can label
    telemetry but must never gate access or be trusted as proof of identity.

  I recommend [1mserver-issued[0m, with the response shape written into the spec as an explicit assumption to validate.
  "Identifies the actual user" and "persists" together point at a real session, and building the persistence layer
  around a self-asserted ID now means redoing every consumer when the backend arrives.
